Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=2 x^{\frac{3}{2}} + 2, \\f'(x)&=3 x^{\frac{1}{2}}=3 \sqrt{x}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(4)&=3 \sqrt{(4)}=6, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(6)}=- \frac{1}{6}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(4,18)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(18)&=- \frac{1}{6}(x-(4)), \\y&=- \frac{x}{6} + \frac{56}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=- \frac{1}{6}x+\left(\frac{56}{3}\right), \\x&=112.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(112,0)$.

